summary:
- topic: FY26 Budget Challenges
  text: The Superintendent presented a challenging outlook for the FY26 budget, noting
    an initial 11.4% local budget increase to maintain current services and legal
    obligations. Factors include reduced reliance on fund balance, exhausted federal
    special education carryover funds, decreased federal title funding, and reduced
    philanthropic support for adult education. The district has implemented a non-essential
    spending freeze and anticipates employee position reductions.
- topic: Pre-K Expansion through Partnerships
  text: Dr. Prince provided an update on pre-K expansion, highlighting a new mixed
    delivery model. This model involves partnerships with private childcare facilities
    like Chickie Toddler Care and United Way of Southern Maine, adding up to 32 free
    pre-K seats at no cost to the local budget, as state funding would pass directly
    to these providers.
- topic: Athletic Complex Referendum Debate
  text: A significant portion of the meeting was dedicated to discussing the proposed
    athletic complex improvements. The Superintendent presented updated costs for
    a November 2025 referendum, with options for two synthetic turf fields and one
    natural grass field, or three natural grass fields, along with a new track, lighting,
    restrooms, and storage. Board members debated the scope, cost, and environmental
    impact of artificial turf versus natural grass, and the necessity of including
    all proposed elements.
- topic: Community Input on Athletic Complex
  text: Citizens expressed concerns about the environmental impact of artificial turf,
    the financial burden on taxpayers (especially seniors), and the perceived bias
    in presentations towards artificial turf. They requested a formal presentation
    from natural grass field installers and emphasized the importance of considering
    long-term costs and environmental sustainability.
- topic: Substitute Motion for Athletic Complex Referendum
  text: 'A substitute motion was introduced and passed, narrowing the scope of the
    referendum to focus on the most critical needs: a new track, the stadium field
    (with options for either synthetic turf or natural grass), permanent bathrooms,
    and lighting. This reduced the overall cost significantly compared to the original
    proposal, aiming to make it more palatable to voters while addressing urgent safety
    and facility needs.'
- topic: 2025-2026 School Calendar Approval
  text: The board approved the 2025-2026 school year calendar, which includes 175
    student days and four staff days. Key features include a lengthy winter break
    and a shift in compensation days for parent communication, with provisions for
    five potential weather days before Juneteenth and a policy against remote learning
    days for snow days unless exceeding five.
- topic: Policy Approvals
  text: Several policies were approved, including the Student Code of Conduct (JIC),
    Job Descriptions (GAB), School Board Legal Status (BB), and Board Member Conflict
    of Interest (BCB). The Policy Committee also noted upcoming revisions to Policy
    EGAD Copyright Compliance and Policy IJOA Field Trips to simplify local walking
    field trip procedures.
